! MCP's HOLLERITH CHANNEL-NUMBER CONVERSION, printed p.17 lines
! 32-36, lifted out of the page and run on its own.
!
! The four rows, as the listing prints them:
!
!   X132    CT0011(V+I)(BU,7), .12($4)   -TEST TENS POSITION OF
!                                         CHANNEL
!           LCV(DU,8,4), $LZC+.3, 46     -CONVERT TO OCTAL NUMBER
!   X132AA  CT0011(V+I)(BU,16), .22($4)  -TEST UNITS POSITION
!           +(BU,4), $LZC+.3, 46         -FORM CHANNEL NUMBER
!
! WHAT IT IS DOING, which took three separate pieces of machinery to
! make runnable and is worth stating once in one place.
!
! A HOLLERITH DIGIT IS A POSITION, NOT A VALUE: one punch among
! several rows of a card column.  CONNECT FOR TEST discards its
! result and sets only the counts, so running it over the column
! leaves the number of zeros to the left of the punch -- THE DIGIT
! ITSELF -- in the left-zeros counter at word 7 bits 17-23
! (Reference Manual printed pp.75-76).  "$LZC+.3" is word 7 bit 20,
! so an eight-bit field there is the counter's low nibble followed by
! four bits of the word beyond it, which are zero: the tens digit
! over a zero.  LOAD CONVERTED reads that as two decimal digits
! (pp.71-73) and leaves tens*10 in the accumulator at offset 46.
! The second CONNECT FOR TEST puts the units digit in the counter,
! and "+(BU,4)" adds the four-bit counter field at the same offset.
!
! Channel number = tens*10 + units, built out of two punch POSITIONS
! by a counter, a radix conversion and an add.
!
! THREE THINGS HAD TO EXIST FOR THIS TO RUN, all built 2026-10-19/20:
! KF (which decides the card is an IOCD card, two rows earlier), LCV,
! and the connective counts.  None of them was reachable before, and
! each on its own was not enough -- building LCV without the counters
! converts nothing, because the counter is where its input comes
! from.
!
! NOTHING IS CHANGED FROM THE PAGE.  An earlier version of this deck
! read two fixed columns and said so; it now walks a card image with
! PROGRESSIVE INDEXING off $4, exactly as the listing writes it,
! because that is the mechanism the real page depends on and leaving
! it out would have been testing the arithmetic while assuming the
! addressing.
!
! HOW THE WALK LANDS, and the first version of this deck got it
! wrong in a way worth keeping.  Under progressive indexing the
! EFFECTIVE ADDRESS IS THE INDEX REGISTER'S OWN VALUE -- the
! instruction's address field is not added to it, it is the
! INCREMENT applied afterwards (Reference Manual printed p.21,
! and StretchExecute's `ea = isProgressive ? xIdx.addr : ...`).
!
! So "CT0011(V+I)(BU,7), .12($4)" reads seven bits AT $4 and then
! advances $4 by twelve; ".22($4)" reads sixteen bits at the new $4
! and advances by twenty-two.  The tens punch therefore sits at card
! bits 0-6 and the units punch at bits 12-27.
!
! The deck was first built with the punches at bits 12-18 and 34-49,
! on the reading that the address field is an offset.  It reported a
! tens digit of 7 -- the all-zero count, because that field was empty
! -- and a units digit of 3, while the index-advance check PASSED.
! That combination is what identified the fault: the walking was
! right and the reading was wrong, which is only distinguishable
! because T3 is checked separately.
!
! THE COLUMNS.  A tens digit of 3 is a punch with three zeros to its
! left in a seven-bit column; a units digit of 5 is a punch with five
! zeros to its left in the sixteen-bit column the page uses for the
! units position.  So the channel number comes out 35.
!
!   T1  after the LOAD CONVERTED: 30, at offset 46.
!   T2  after the add: 35, at offset 46.  **T1 is checked as well as
!       T2 because the two halves fail differently** -- a broken
!       counter gives the wrong tens AND the wrong units, while a
!       broken conversion leaves T1 wrong and T2 wrong by the same
!       amount.  Seeing both says which half moved.
!   T3  where the walk left $4: the card's address plus 12 plus 22
!       bits.  Checked because the two connectives' job is half
!       arithmetic and half ADVANCING, and a progressive index that
!       did not advance would still give the right channel number
!       here -- both columns would simply be read from where they
!       are -- while leaving MCP's loop reading the same card
!       forever.

! ONE INJECTION, MEASURED on this version of the deck: LOAD CONVERTED
! made to copy the digit bits instead of converting them reads the
! eight-bit field 0x30 as binary 48 rather than decimal 30, giving
! T1 = 0xc000000000000 and T2 = 0xd400000000000 -- the channel number
! comes out 53 instead of 35.  The two value checks move by the same
! arithmetic and the index-advance check does not, which is the split
! between reading the card and walking it.

        SLC, (8)2000.0

START   LVI, $X4, CARD               ! point $4 at the card image
        CT0011(V+I)(BU,7), .12($X4)   ! -TEST TENS POSITION OF CHANNEL
        LCV(DU,8,4), $LZC+.3, 46      ! -CONVERT TO OCTAL NUMBER
        ST(BU,64,8), T1, 0            ! (not in the page: look at it)
        CT0011(V+I)(BU,16), .22($X4)  ! -TEST UNITS POSITION
        +(BU,4), $LZC+.3, 46          ! -FORM CHANNEL NUMBER
        ST(BU,64,8), T2, 0
        SX, $X4, T3                   ! where the walk left $4

PASS    BEW, $

! Data, word-aligned: BEW is a half word, so without this the 64-bit
! fields below would straddle two words.
        SLC, (8)2020.0

! The card image: a seven-bit tens column at bits 0-6 with three
! zeros to the left of its punch, and a sixteen-bit units column at
! bits 12-27 with five.
CARD    DD(BU,64,8), (8)0100002000000000000000

T1      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T2      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T3      DD(BU,64,8), 0

        END, START
